<!DOCTYPE html>
<html class="antialiased" lang="en" data-theme="light" style="font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />

    <!-- Primary Meta Tags -->
    <title>Dyslexia Font Tester: OpenDyslexic vs Lexend vs Atkinson - Coffee &amp; Fun LLC</title>
    <meta name="title" content="Dyslexia Font Tester: OpenDyslexic vs Lexend vs Atkinson - Coffee & Fun LLC" />
    <meta name="description" content="Paste your text and compare OpenDyslexic, Lexend, Atkinson Hyperlegible and a standard sans side by side, with live letter spacing, word spacing, line height and line length." />
    <meta name="author" content="Coffee & Fun LLC" />
    <meta name="robots" content="index, follow" />

    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.coffeeandfun.com/dyslexia-font-tester/" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/images/brand/favicon.png" sizes="any" />

    <!-- Open Graph -->
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Coffee & Fun" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Dyslexia Font Tester: OpenDyslexic vs Lexend vs Atkinson" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="Compare OpenDyslexic, Lexend, Atkinson Hyperlegible and a standard sans on your own text, with live spacing and line length controls." />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://www.coffeeandfun.com/dyslexia-font-tester/" />
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://www.coffeeandfun.com/assets/images/social/pages/dyslexia-font-tester.png" />
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
    <meta property="og:image:alt" content="Dyslexia font tester. OpenDyslexic vs Lexend vs Atkinson" />
    <meta property="og:locale" content="en_US" />

    <!-- Twitter Card -->
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:site" content="@bycoffeeandfun" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="Dyslexia Font Tester: OpenDyslexic vs Lexend vs Atkinson" />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="Compare OpenDyslexic, Lexend, Atkinson Hyperlegible and a standard sans on your own text, with live spacing and line length controls." />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://www.coffeeandfun.com/assets/images/social/pages/dyslexia-font-tester.png" />
    <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="Dyslexia font tester. OpenDyslexic vs Lexend vs Atkinson" />

    <meta name="theme-color" content="#3d2b1f" />

    <script type="application/ld+json">
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@graph": [
          {
            "@type": "WebApplication",
            "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/dyslexia-font-tester/#app",
            "name": "Dyslexia Font Tester",
            "alternateName": "OpenDyslexic vs Lexend vs Atkinson Hyperlegible comparison",
            "description": "Compare OpenDyslexic, Lexend, Atkinson Hyperlegible and a standard sans-serif on your own text, with live controls for letter spacing, word spacing, line height and line length.",
            "url": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/dyslexia-font-tester/",
            "applicationCategory": "DeveloperApplication",
            "operatingSystem": "Web Browser",
            "browserRequirements": "Requires JavaScript",
            "isAccessibleForFree": true,
            "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" },
            "publisher": { "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/#organization" },
            "featureList": [
              "Compare four typefaces on the same text at identical settings",
              "Live letter spacing, word spacing, line height and line length controls",
              "One click WCAG 2.1 text spacing preset",
              "Focus mode to read a single typeface at full width",
              "Shareable link that reproduces your exact settings",
              "Runs entirely in the browser with no upload"
            ]
          },
          {
            "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
            "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/dyslexia-font-tester/#breadcrumb",
            "itemListElement": [
              { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/" },
              { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Apps and tools", "item": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/apps/" },
              { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Dyslexia Font Tester", "item": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/dyslexia-font-tester/" }
            ]
          },
          {
            "@type": "FAQPage",
            "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/dyslexia-font-tester/#faq",
            "mainEntity": [
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Does OpenDyslexic actually help people with dyslexia?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "The evidence is mixed. Controlled studies have generally not found that dyslexia-specific typefaces improve reading speed or accuracy compared with a plain sans-serif, while changes to spacing and line length have shown more consistent effects. Plenty of readers still report preferring it, and preference matters, which is why this tool lets you compare rather than telling you an answer."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Which font should I use on my website?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "For body text on a public site, a well spaced standard sans-serif is usually the safer default, because it is already installed everywhere and costs no download. Spend your effort on line height, line length and letter spacing first. If you want a typeface designed for character distinction, Atkinson Hyperlegible is a strong choice and is free to embed."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What is WCAG 1.4.12 text spacing?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Success Criterion 1.4.12 of WCAG 2.1 says content must stay readable and functional when a reader overrides spacing to at least 1.5 times line height, 0.12em letter spacing, 0.16em word spacing and 2em paragraph spacing. The preset button in this tool applies those values so you can see whether your text survives them."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Are these fonts free to use commercially?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Yes. OpenDyslexic, Lexend and Atkinson Hyperlegible are all released under the SIL Open Font License 1.1, which permits commercial use and embedding. Keep the license and copyright notice with the font files when you redistribute them."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Is my text uploaded anywhere?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "No. Everything runs in your browser. Nothing you paste is sent to a server, logged or stored. The only time your text leaves the page is if you choose to copy the share link, which encodes short text into the URL itself."
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    </script>

    {% include schema-org.liquid %}

    {% bundledCss %}
    {% pageCss "dyslexia-font-tester" %}
</head>

<body class="bg-cream">
    {% include header.liquid %}

    {% include tool-hero.liquid %}

    <main id="main-content">



        <!-- Tool -->
        <section class="dft-app px-4 py-10 sm:px-6 sm:py-14" data-dft-app>
            <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-7xl gap-6 lg:grid-cols-[22rem_1fr] lg:items-start">

                <!-- Controls -->
                <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[#ecdcc7] bg-[#fffcf7] p-6 shadow-sm lg:sticky lg:top-32">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-cocoa">Your text</h2>
                    <label class="sr-only" for="dft-text">Text to preview</label>
                    <textarea id="dft-text" data-dft-text rows="6"
                        class="textarea mt-3 w-full rounded-xl border-[#ddc9ae] bg-white text-sm text-cocoa"
                        placeholder="Paste any text to preview it"></textarea>

                    <h2 class="mt-8 text-lg font-bold text-cocoa">Spacing</h2>
                    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-[#5c4a3a]">Applied to all four typefaces at once, so the only thing that differs between the panels is the typeface itself.</p>

                    <div class="mt-5 space-y-5">
                        <div>
                            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
                                <label class="text-sm font-semibold text-cocoa" for="dft-size">Text size</label>
                                <output class="text-sm font-mono text-[#5c4a3a]" data-dft-out="size" for="dft-size">18px</output>
                            </div>
                            <input id="dft-size" class="range dft-range mt-2 w-full" data-dft-slider="size"
                                type="range" min="12" max="40" step="1" value="18" />
                        </div>

                        <div>
                            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
                                <label class="text-sm font-semibold text-cocoa" for="dft-letter">Letter spacing</label>
                                <output class="text-sm font-mono text-[#5c4a3a]" data-dft-out="letter" for="dft-letter">0em</output>
                            </div>
                            <input id="dft-letter" class="range dft-range mt-2 w-full" data-dft-slider="letter"
                                type="range" min="0" max="0.4" step="0.01" value="0" />
                        </div>

                        <div>
                            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
                                <label class="text-sm font-semibold text-cocoa" for="dft-word">Word spacing</label>
                                <output class="text-sm font-mono text-[#5c4a3a]" data-dft-out="word" for="dft-word">0em</output>
                            </div>
                            <input id="dft-word" class="range dft-range mt-2 w-full" data-dft-slider="word"
                                type="range" min="0" max="0.6" step="0.01" value="0" />
                        </div>

                        <div>
                            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
                                <label class="text-sm font-semibold text-cocoa" for="dft-line">Line height</label>
                                <output class="text-sm font-mono text-[#5c4a3a]" data-dft-out="line" for="dft-line">1.5</output>
                            </div>
                            <input id="dft-line" class="range dft-range mt-2 w-full" data-dft-slider="line"
                                type="range" min="1" max="3" step="0.05" value="1.5" />
                        </div>

                        <div>
                            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
                                <label class="text-sm font-semibold text-cocoa" for="dft-measure">Line length</label>
                                <output class="text-sm font-mono text-[#5c4a3a]" data-dft-out="measure" for="dft-measure">65 characters</output>
                            </div>
                            <input id="dft-measure" class="range dft-range mt-2 w-full" data-dft-slider="measure"
                                type="range" min="20" max="120" step="1" value="65" />
                            <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-[#5c4a3a]">The indigo bar above each sample shows where the line breaks.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
                        <button type="button" data-dft-wcag
                            class="btn btn-sm rounded-full border-[#e3d2ba] bg-[#f6ecdd] text-espresso hover:bg-[#efe0cb]">
                            Apply WCAG 1.4.12 spacing
                        </button>
                        <button type="button" data-dft-reset
                            class="btn btn-sm btn-ghost rounded-full text-espresso">Reset</button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="mt-8">
                        <label class="text-sm font-semibold text-cocoa" for="dft-focus">Show</label>
                        <select id="dft-focus" data-dft-focus
                            class="select mt-2 w-full rounded-xl border-[#ddc9ae] bg-white text-sm text-cocoa">
                            <option value="">All four typefaces</option>
                            <option value="system">Standard sans only</option>
                            <option value="opendyslexic">OpenDyslexic only</option>
                            <option value="lexend">Lexend only</option>
                            <option value="atkinson">Atkinson Hyperlegible only</option>
                        </select>
                    </div>

                    <div class="mt-8 border-t border-[#ecdcc7] pt-6">
                        <label class="text-sm font-semibold text-cocoa" for="dft-share">Share these settings</label>
                        <input id="dft-share" data-dft-share readonly
                            class="input mt-2 w-full rounded-xl border-[#ddc9ae] bg-cream font-mono text-xs text-[#4a3a2c]" />
                        <button type="button" data-dft-copy
                            class="btn btn-sm mt-2 rounded-full bg-espresso text-cream hover:bg-cocoa">Copy link</button>
                        <span class="ml-2 text-sm font-medium text-emerald-700" data-dft-share-msg role="status"></span>
                    </div>

                    <p class="sr-only" data-dft-status role="status"></p>
                </div>

                <!-- Samples -->
                <div class="grid gap-5 xl:grid-cols-2">
                    <article class="dft-panel rounded-2xl border border-[#ecdcc7] bg-[#fffcf7] p-6 shadow-sm" data-font="system">
                        <div class="mb-4">
                            <h2 class="text-base font-bold text-cocoa">Standard sans <span class="ml-1 rounded-full bg-[#f3e7d8] px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-semibold text-[#5c4a3a]">Control</span></h2>
                            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-[#5c4a3a]">Whatever your system already uses. No download, no setup.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="dft-ruler mb-3" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                        <p class="dft-sample dft-f-system" data-dft-sample></p>
                    </article>

                    <article class="dft-panel rounded-2xl border border-[#ecdcc7] bg-[#fffcf7] p-6 shadow-sm" data-font="opendyslexic">
                        <div class="mb-4">
                            <h2 class="text-base font-bold text-cocoa">OpenDyslexic</h2>
                            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-[#5c4a3a]">Weighted bottoms, meant to stop letters flipping. By Abbie Gonzalez.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="dft-ruler mb-3" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                        <p class="dft-sample dft-f-opendyslexic" data-dft-sample></p>
                    </article>

                    <article class="dft-panel rounded-2xl border border-[#ecdcc7] bg-[#fffcf7] p-6 shadow-sm" data-font="lexend">
                        <div class="mb-4">
                            <h2 class="text-base font-bold text-cocoa">Lexend</h2>
                            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-[#5c4a3a]">Built around reading proficiency rather than letter shape alone.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="dft-ruler mb-3" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                        <p class="dft-sample dft-f-lexend" data-dft-sample></p>
                    </article>

                    <article class="dft-panel rounded-2xl border border-[#ecdcc7] bg-[#fffcf7] p-6 shadow-sm" data-font="atkinson">
                        <div class="mb-4">
                            <h2 class="text-base font-bold text-cocoa">Atkinson Hyperlegible</h2>
                            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-[#5c4a3a]">Designed for low vision by the Braille Institute. Pulls apart I, l and 1.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="dft-ruler mb-3" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                        <p class="dft-sample dft-f-atkinson" data-dft-sample></p>
                    </article>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Reference -->
        <section class="border-t border-[#ecdcc7] bg-[#fffcf7] px-6 py-16">
            <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-cocoa">What the research actually says</h2>
                <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-[#4a3a2c]">
                    Dyslexia-specific typefaces are marketed with more confidence than the evidence
                    supports. Controlled studies have generally failed to show that they improve
                    reading speed or accuracy against a plain sans-serif, while adjustments to
                    spacing and line length have produced more consistent results.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-[#4a3a2c]">
                    That is worth knowing before you swap a typeface and call the job done. It is
                    also why the spacing controls here apply to all four panels at once: if the text
                    got easier to read, you should be able to tell whether it was the typeface or
                    the spacing that did it.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-[#4a3a2c]">
                    None of which means preference does not count. A reader who finds one typeface
                    more comfortable will read more of it, and that is a real result even when a
                    stopwatch cannot see it. The point of comparing is to find out what works for
                    you, not to be handed a winner.
                </p>

                <!-- TODO: Robert, add the specific study citations here when the "Do Dyslexia Fonts
                     Work?" post lands (queue item 2). I have kept the claims general on purpose
                     rather than attach numbers I have not checked. -->

                <h2 class="mt-14 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-cocoa">Start with these four things</h2>
                <ol class="mt-6 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-[#4a3a2c] marker:font-bold marker:text-caramel">
                    <li><strong>Line height of at least 1.5.</strong> The single change with the best return, and the one most sites get wrong.</li>
                    <li><strong>Line length of 60 to 75 characters.</strong> Long lines make it hard to find the start of the next one.</li>
                    <li><strong>Letter spacing around 0.12em.</strong> The value WCAG 1.4.12 expects your layout to survive.</li>
                    <li><strong>Left aligned, never justified.</strong> Justification opens rivers of white space that pull the eye down instead of across.</li>
                </ol>

                <h2 class="mt-14 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-cocoa">Questions</h2>
                <div class="mt-6 space-y-3">
                    <details class="group rounded-xl border border-[#ecdcc7] bg-cream p-5">
                        <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-semibold text-cocoa">Does OpenDyslexic actually help?</summary>
                        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#4a3a2c]">The evidence is mixed, and mostly not in its favour on speed and accuracy. Plenty of readers still prefer it, and preference is not nothing. Compare it here on your own text rather than taking anyone's word for it, including ours.</p>
                    </details>
                    <details class="group rounded-xl border border-[#ecdcc7] bg-cream p-5">
                        <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-semibold text-cocoa">Which font should I use on my site?</summary>
                        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#4a3a2c]">For body text, a well spaced standard sans is usually the safer default. It is already installed, so it costs nothing to download and never flashes an unstyled fallback. Spend the effort on line height, line length and letter spacing first. If you do want a designed-for-legibility face, Atkinson Hyperlegible is a strong pick and free to embed.</p>
                    </details>
                    <details class="group rounded-xl border border-[#ecdcc7] bg-cream p-5">
                        <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-semibold text-cocoa">What is WCAG 1.4.12 text spacing?</summary>
                        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#4a3a2c]">A WCAG 2.1 success criterion. Your content has to stay readable and functional when a reader forces line height to 1.5, letter spacing to 0.12em, word spacing to 0.16em and paragraph spacing to 2em. The preset button applies those values so you can check whether your layout holds up.</p>
                    </details>
                    <details class="group rounded-xl border border-[#ecdcc7] bg-cream p-5">
                        <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-semibold text-cocoa">Are these fonts free to use commercially?</summary>
                        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#4a3a2c]">Yes. All three are under the SIL Open Font License 1.1, which allows commercial use and embedding. Keep the licence and copyright notice with the files when you redistribute them.</p>
                    </details>
                    <details class="group rounded-xl border border-[#ecdcc7] bg-cream p-5">
                        <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-semibold text-cocoa">Is my text uploaded anywhere?</summary>
                        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#4a3a2c]">No. It all runs in your browser. Nothing is sent to a server, logged or stored. The only time your text leaves this page is if you copy the share link, which packs short text into the URL itself.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="mt-14 rounded-2xl border border-[#ecdcc7] bg-cream p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-base font-bold text-cocoa">Font credits</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[#5c4a3a]">
                        OpenDyslexic (c) Abbie Gonzalez. Lexend (c) The Lexend Project Authors.
                        Atkinson Hyperlegible (c) Braille Institute of America, Inc. All three are
                        licensed under the SIL Open Font License 1.1 and are used here unmodified.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-[#5c4a3a]">
                        Checking colours as well as type? Try the
                        <a class="font-semibold text-espresso underline underline-offset-2" href="/contrast-checker/">colour contrast checker</a>,
                        or see all our <a class="font-semibold text-espresso underline underline-offset-2" href="/apps/">free tools</a>.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

    </main>

    {% include modals.liquid %}
    {% include footer.liquid %}

    {% pageJs "dyslexia-font-tester" %}
</body>
</html>
